---
title: Boiled eggs, soft to hard
description: Lesson 0.3. Six eggs, three timings, and calibrating your own kitchen.
servings: 3
time: 30 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, basics]
lesson: "0.3"
technique: boiling eggs
status: draft
---

> Technique: timing eggs. An egg is a sealed experiment: you cannot
> look inside or take its temperature, so time is the only control.
> This lesson cooks six eggs and takes them out in pairs, so you learn
> what your own hob, pot and eggs do.

Fill a #bowl{} with cold water and @ice{2%handfuls}.

Bring 3 cm of water to a rolling boil in a #saucepan{}(with a lid). Lower in @eggs{6}(large, cold from the fridge) with a spoon, cover, and turn the heat down to medium so the water keeps boiling gently. Start a timer.

> Science: start hot, not cold. Dropped into boiling water or steam,
> the outermost proteins of the white set at once and shrink away
> from the membrane under the shell. Heated slowly from cold, they
> have time to stick to the membrane first, and the egg peels badly.
> In the test kitchen's trials the hot start peeled cleanly with
> fresh and old eggs alike. Look up: protein denaturation, egg shell
> membrane.
-- source: Andrea Geary, "Easy-Peel Hard-Cooked Eggs", America's Test Kitchen, 2016-03-01, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/168-easy-peel-hard-cooked-eggs

After ~{7%minutes}, lift out two eggs and put them in the ice water. After ~{3%minutes} more, take out two more. After another ~{3%minutes}, 13 minutes in all, take out the last two.

> Why ice water: an egg keeps cooking in its own heat after it leaves
> the pot. The ice stops it where you meant it to stop.
-- source: Andrea Geary, "Easy-Peel Hard-Cooked Eggs", America's Test Kitchen, 2016-03-01, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/168-easy-peel-hard-cooked-eggs

Leave the eggs in the ice water for ~{5%minutes}, then peel one of each and cut it in half. Compare the three yolks and write down which time you like.

> Science: the white is a mixture of proteins that set at different
> temperatures: ovotransferrin, 12% of its protein, at about 61 to
> 65 °C, and ovalbumin, 54% of it, at about 75 to 84 °C. Heat moves in
> from the shell, so the white reaches those temperatures long before
> the yolk does, and time decides how far the heat gets. These
> sources do not give figures for the yolk. Look up: ovalbumin,
> ovotransferrin, coagulation.
-- source: Mannott, Bosse and Gibis, "Recent Advances in Egg White Biotechnology, Microbiology, and Quality", Comprehensive Reviews in Food Science and Food Safety 25:e70495, 2026, https://doi.org/10.1111/1541-4337.70495 (Table 1)

> Safety: the 13 minute egg, firm all through, is the one that meets
> official advice. The US FDA says to cook eggs until both yolk and
> white are firm. A soft yolk is below that advice, which matters most
> for children, older adults, pregnant women and people with weakened
> immune systems.
-- source: US Food and Drug Administration, "What You Need to Know About Egg Safety", content current as of 2024-03-05, https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/what-you-need-know-about-egg-safety

Serve with @salt{} and @buttered toast{3%slices}.

> Next time, without the recipe: cold eggs into boiling water, lid on,
> a timer, and ice water at the end. Thirteen minutes is the test
> kitchen's time for a firm yolk; the shorter times are yours to
> calibrate.
-- source: Andrea Geary, "Easy-Peel Hard-Boiled Eggs", America's Test Kitchen, 2016-01-22, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/recipes/8712-easy-peel-hard-cooked-eggs (13 minutes, in steam over 1 inch of boiling water; the 7 and 10 minute stops in this lesson are untested starting points)
